{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport json\ns=json.load(open('/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-19_13-32-32-dragonboat_raft-real-run/state.json'))\nfor it in s['interpretations']:\n    if it['id']=='545f390aeead46dc8d1f3abd858fd5e3-interpretation-0':\n        print(json.dumps(it,indent=1))\nEOF\"","aggregated_output":"{\n \"id\": \"545f390aeead46dc8d1f3abd858fd5e3-interpretation-0\",\n \"investigation_id\": \"545f390aeead46dc8d1f3abd858fd5e3-question-0\",\n \"experiment_id\": null,\n \"source_ids\": [\n  \"raft_restore_snapshot_gate\",\n  \"logentry_restore_snapshot\",\n  \"inmemory_restore_snapshot\",\n  \"inmemory_clear_snapshot\",\n  \"peer_update_snapshot_field\",\n  \"peer_update_commit_snapshot\",\n  \"node_process_snapshot\",\n  \"node_push_snapshot\",\n  \"node_entries_to_apply\",\n  \"rsm_handle_snapshot_boundary\",\n  \"node_process_recover_status\",\n  \"rsm_recover_out_of_date_guard\",\n  \"engine_process_steps_order\",\n  \"engine_apply_snapshot_and_update\",\n  \"node_push_entries\"\n ],\n \"executions\": {},\n \"conclusion\": \"explained\",\n \"defect_claim\": \"refuted\",\n \"rationale\": \"Current claim for this question: a snapshot install leaves the node with one applied frontier, and the suspicion that committed entries could be replayed on top of recovered state or that later entries could be applied ahead of the recovery is refuted for the traced paths. Basis, in the order the node reaches it. (1) The raft layer adopts a snapshot only when its index is above the committed index, so the snapshot index is greater than every entry index the node has ever pushed to its state machine; a snapshot at or below committed is refused without being attached to any update, and when the log already holds the snapshot's index and term the log is not replaced and only the commit index moves. (2) An adopted snapshot is carried in every update from that peer until that update commits, and the update commit sets StableSnapshotTo to the snapshot index, raises Processed to at least that index and clears the in-memory snapshot pointer, so the same adopted snapshot is not redelivered by the peer. (3) In the engine, a snapshot update is marked not fast-appliable, is skipped by the first pass, and is processed only after the raft state write and the snapshot flag removal; processSnapshot records the snapshot in the log reader and pushSnapshot queues a Recover task, then applyRaftUpdates filters the same update's committed entries against the new pushed index. (4) entriesToApply drops every entry at or below pushedIndex, panics rather than silently skipping when the batch ends below it or starts beyond pushedIndex+1, and the union with (1) means an adopted snapshot can never present entries that are both above the frontier and separated from it by a hole. (5) Ordering is carried by the queue, not by a lock: the state machine applies entries queued before the recovery task, returns the recovery task to the apply loop when it reaches it, and refuses to handle anything queued behind it because the recovering flag makes processStatusTransition report the cluster as busy; the flag is cleared only after the recovery completion is read. (6) A recovery that finds the state machine already at or beyond the snapshot index is reported as an out-of-date snapshot and treated as an aborted recovery rather than an error, so even a recovery task that did reach the worker with a satisfied index ends without changing the frontier. The asymmetric part is deliberate and bounded: pushedIndex and the node's snapshot index move at queue time, while the state machine state moves at recovery completion, and the recovering gate is what covers that interval.\",\n \"answers\": {},\n \"knowledge\": [],\n \"counterevidence\": [\n  \"pushSnapshot sets pushedIndex and the node's snapshot index when the recovery task is queued, so for the duration of the asynchronous recovery the node's recorded frontier is ahead of the state machine's actual applied index; only the recovering flag and the queue order keep committed entries from being offered in that window.\",\n  \"Committed entries queued before the snapshot are applied to the state machine before the recovery task is handled and are then overwritten by the recovered snapshot state, so a snapshot install re-executes those updates rather than skipping them; this is idempotent only because the recovery replaces state at the snapshot index.\",\n  \"node.pushSnapshot treats a snapshot index equal to pushedIndex, to the node's snapshot index or to the applied index as acceptable and only panics when it is strictly lower, so the equal-index case relies on the raft layer's committed gate and on the state machine's out-of-date recovery result rather than on pushSnapshot's own comparison.\"\n ],\n \"limitations\": [\n  \"Source reading only in this account; the submitted probe has not produced a retained receipt yet, so no execution observation is cited here.\",\n  \"The trace follows a native state machine installed on a running follower; the on-disk state machine recovery branch, which may skip loading a snapshot it already holds, is not traced here.\",\n  \"The snapshotter's own file lifecycle (flag files, compaction and shrink) is only used as a precondition of the recovery task and is not investigated in this pass.\",\n  \"Restart and offload paths that reconstruct the frontier through replayLog and setInitialStatus are outside this trace, so the claim is about a live follower receiving a snapshot.\"\n ],\n \"joint_history\": \"\",\n \"failure_attribution\": null,\n \"operation_id\": \"545f390aeead46dc8d1f3abd858fd5e3\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
